<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manfred, King of Sicily</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1232">1232</a>–<a href="/years/1266">1266</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Manfred of Sicily · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manfred was a son of Emperor Frederick II and king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from 1258 to 1266. Opposed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, sending knights to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelagonia">Battle of Pelagonia</a> in 1259. He was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-benevento">Battle of Benevento</a> in 1266 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a>, who took his kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manfred-king-of-sicily.jpg" alt="Manfred, King of Sicily" width="244" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frederick II (Hohenstaufen) / Manfred of Sicily (Hohenstaufen) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manfred_Sizilien_Manfredi_Sicilia_De_Arte_Venandi_cum_Avibus_-_dated_MSS_1258-1266.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Manfred was born in 1232 at Venosa, a natural son of Emperor Frederick II, who seems to have regarded him as legitimate and named him Prince of Taranto in his will. He studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> and shared his father's love of poetry and science. After Frederick's death in 1250 he acted as regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> while his half-brother Conrad IV was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. When Conrad came to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1252 he stripped Manfred of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> except Taranto. Conrad died of malaria in May 1254, and Manfred took the regency for the infant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conradin">Conradin</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a> excommunicated him in July 1254. Manfred submitted in September and accepted the title of papal vicar, but he fled to the Saracens at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucera">Lucera</a>, and with their help defeated the papal army at Foggia on 2 December 1254. By 1257 he had crushed another papal army and put down the rebellions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On 10 August 1258, on a rumour that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conradin">Conradin</a> was dead, Manfred was crowned king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>. The pope declared the coronation void, but Manfred named vicars in Tuscany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>. After Montaperti he was recognised as protector of Tuscany by the citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, and in 1262 his daughter Constance married the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-aragon">Peter III of Aragon</a>. He also took Helena Angelina Doukaina, daughter of the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, as his second wife.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-iv">Pope Urban IV</a> excommunicated him and in 1263 gave the investiture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a>, a brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a>. Charles entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in late 1265 with an army of some 30,000 and was crowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in January 1266. The armies met at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-benevento">Battle of Benevento</a> on 26 February 1266. Manfred refused to flee, rushed into the midst of his enemies and was killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Manfred was buried on the battlefield under a heap of stones, but with the pope's consent his remains were dug up and interred on the bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garigliano">Garigliano</a>, outside the territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and the Papal States. Charles imprisoned Helena, who died in 1271, and kept her sons in chains for decades. Peter III eventually became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 1282 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">Sicilian Vespers</a>. Manfred founded the modern city of Manfredonia between 1256 and 1263, and in the Divine Comedy Dante meets him outside the gates of Purgatory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manfred, King of Sicily” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The following year, allied with Michael II of Epirus and <b>Manfred of Sicily</b> against the Empire of Nicaea, William was defeated and captured at the Battle of Pelagonia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Benevento in southern Italy, the army of Charles I of Anjou defeated <b>Manfred, King of Sicily</b>, who was killed in the battle.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-26">26 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Manfred of Sicily</b> married Helena Angelina Doukaina, daughter of Michael II Komnenos Doukas, Despot of Epirus, as an expression of their alliance against the power of the Empire of Nicaea.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-2">2 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Siena, Sienese Ghibellines, Florentine exiles under Farinata degli Uberti and German cavalry sent by King <b>Manfred of Sicily</b> routed the Guelph army of Florence.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-4">4 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Venosa, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.97,15.82&amp;year=1254" aria-label="Venosa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Manfred, King of Sicily"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4093 4011h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4093 4011h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.97,15.82&amp;year=1254">What was here in 1254? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
